# Line endings and binary safety for a Word VBA project.
#
# The VBE is a CRLF tool at both ends: Export-Macros.ps1 writes the modules in
# src/ with CRLF, and Import-Macros.ps1 hands those same files back to
# VBComponents.Import. Normalizing to LF in the repo and checking out CRLF means
# the working tree matches what the VBE expects on every machine, whatever
# core.autocrlf happens to be set to there -- rather than leaving that to a
# per-clone setting nobody remembers to check.
*.bas   text eol=crlf
*.cls   text eol=crlf
*.frm   text eol=crlf

# The form's resource file is an OLE compound document, not text. Git's own
# heuristic already reads it as binary on the strength of its NUL bytes, so this
# line changes nothing today -- it is here so the form does not depend on that
# heuristic continuing to fire. One LF rewritten to CRLF inside it corrupts the
# form, and a corrupt .frx fails at import, which surfaces as a compile error in
# a module that has nothing wrong with it.
*.frx   binary

# Windows scripts, and the .dotm if one is ever committed deliberately.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.dotm  binary

*.md    text
